# demo: performance and cost report

What does adopting this package add to latency, CPU, memory and compute spend -- and how does that grow with workload? Three views, from the whole to the part: **(1)** the end-to-end total, **(2)** every function on its own, **(3)** which functions make up the total. New to benchmarks? Read [READING-BENCHMARKS.md](READING-BENCHMARKS.md) first.

## What adopting this package costs

_The end-to-end benchmark produced no complete result at the typical workload size, so no cost summary is available. See the failed/skipped section._

## 1. End-to-end: the package's total impact

Measures what routing the workload through the package adds.

- **Baseline (no package):** Process items directly.
- **With the package:** Process items via the package.

Both sides use empty or minimal functions on purpose, so the difference is the package's own cost -- not the cost of the work an application would plug into it. Real applications add their own work on top; this is the floor the package imposes.

**Variables that could change this result**

| Variable | How it is handled | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| items | swept | The tier axis. |
| mode | fixed at "strict" | Validation mode. |

The baseline is an empty or minimal function, so it costs almost nothing and the _relative_ overhead can look enormous (shown as a multiple of the baseline). Read the absolute columns -- time, CPU and dollars added -- they are what a bill and a latency budget are made of.

| items | Baseline | With package | Added | Added vs baseline | Added CPU | Est. $ / 1M ops |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |

## 2. Function by function

Every function the package exposes is measured on its own across the full size ladder, then its measured growth rate is compared with the big-O we documented for it. A function whose measured class **differs** from its documented one is the most useful thing in this report: either the documentation or the code is wrong.

| Function | Documented | Measured | Agreement | At 80 | At 160 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `sum` | O(n) | O(1) | ⚠️ differs | 1.00 ms | 1.00 ms |
| `lookup` | O(1) | O(1) | ✅ matches | 1.00 ms | 1.00 ms |

> **Needs attention.** These functions grow at a different rate than documented. Either the code regressed or the documentation is wrong -- decide which, then fix it:
>
> - `sum`: documented O(n), measured O(1) (exponent 0.00)

### `sum`

**Why we benchmark it.** Adds the items up, once per operation.

**What poor performance would mean.** Every operation pays for the slow sum.

**Expected growth: O(n).** A sufficiently long and honest explanation.

**Variables that could change its cost**

| Variable | How it is handled | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| items | swept | The tier axis. |

**In the end-to-end run:** Called twice per operation.

**Measured: O(1)** (exponent 0.00, 4 sizes) -- ⚠️ differs.

| items | Median | p95 | CPU (median) | Heap Δ | Ops/s |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 20 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 200 B | 1,000 |
| 40 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 400 B | 1,000 |
| 80 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 800 B | 1,000 |
| 160 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 1.6 KiB | 1,000 |

### `lookup`

**Why we benchmark it.** Finds one item by key.

**What poor performance would mean.** Lookups slow down as the set grows.

**Expected growth: O(1).** A hash lookup does not depend on the number of items.

**Variables that could change its cost**

| Variable | How it is handled | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| keys | fixed at 1 | A single key per lookup. |

**In the end-to-end run:** Called once per operation.

**Measured: O(1)** (exponent 0.00, 4 sizes) -- ✅ matches.

| items | Median | p95 | CPU (median) | Heap Δ | Ops/s |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 20 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 200 B | 1,000 |
| 40 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 400 B | 1,000 |
| 80 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 800 B | 1,000 |
| 160 | 1.00 ms | 1.20 ms | 500 µs | 1.6 KiB | 1,000 |

## 3. What makes up one end-to-end operation

Each function's measured cost is multiplied by how many times one end-to-end operation calls it. A function measured on its own includes everything it calls, so where one attributed function calls another, the nested time is subtracted from the caller (its _exclusive_ time): every moment of the operation belongs to at most one row. Shares are measured against the whole operation with the package, a figure measured directly -- not against the _added_ time, which is the small difference of two noisy medians. They are estimates (each function was measured separately); the remainder is shown as _unattributed_.

